  <data name="Info_Paste_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When you copy (Ctrl+C) or cut (Ctrl+X) files and paste them (Ctrl+V) in File Explorer or on the desktop, MegaCopier does the copy instead of Windows: with a queue, pause, verification and the option to resume if it is interrupted.\n• Big copies to an external drive: you can pause them and continue later.\n• Several copies in a row: they queue up instead of fighting over the disk.\nIf you paste into the same folder (to duplicate a file), File Explorer still does it.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Drag_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When you drag files with the left button from one window to another, MegaCopier copies (or moves) them with the same rules as Windows: on the same drive it moves, Ctrl forces a copy and Shift forces a move.\n• Sort photos by dragging them between folders on different drives.\n• Move big projects without freezing File Explorer.\nDropping onto the folders and drives in the left pane works too. Dropping onto a file or a ZIP is still handled by File Explorer.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_WindowMode_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The copy window has three views.\n• Small: just the bars and buttons. It’s the usual one while copying; you can make it wider, but not taller.\n• With the list (“More”): files, destination, options, graph and log. It can be enlarged or maximized.\n• Several copies: the same, with the list of copies on the left; it appears by itself when there’s more than one.\n“Automatic” starts small and switches to the full view when several copies run at once. “More” and “Less” switch views at any time.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Verify_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When each file finishes, MegaCopier reads it back straight from the destination disk and compares its fingerprint (hash) with the original’s. If anything doesn’t match, it re-copies only the damaged part.\n• Backups, photos and important documents.\n• Old or cheap USB sticks and drives, which can fail without warning.\n• Copies over the network or Wi-Fi.\nThe copy takes a bit longer (everything has to be read again), in exchange for knowing it is identical.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Conflict_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What to do when the destination already has a file with the same name.\n• Ask: shows you both, with their size, date and a thumbnail, and you choose.\n• Replace, Skip, Replace if newer or if the size differs, Keep both (&quot;name (2)&quot;) or Rename the existing one.\nIdentical files (same size and date) are never copied again: repeating a copy only copies what is new or changed. If a whole folder already exists, MegaCopier asks before starting.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Hash_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The hash is like a file’s fingerprint: if a single byte changes, the fingerprint changes. It is used when verifying and in saved checksums.\n• xxHash3: the fastest; ideal for verifying copies.\n• SHA-256: the one websites and programs use most to check downloads.\n• MD5, SHA-1, BLAKE3…: in case another program asks for a specific one.\nFor everyday use, keep xxHash3.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Performance_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How fast the copy can go.\n• Speed limit: so the copy doesn’t eat up the whole network or disk while you work or watch a film. Each copy also has its own limit in its Options tab, which changes right away without stopping it.\n• Buffer size: the blocks read and written at a time. Larger is better for huge files.\n• Operations at once: how many blocks are requested from the disk at the same time. Fast SSDs gain with more.\nIf you’re not sure, keep the defaults: MegaCopier already tunes each disk by itself.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_MoveAttributes_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When moving to another disk, files are copied and then deleted from the source. Here you choose what they take along besides the content: attributes (read-only, hidden…), permissions, owner and auditing.\n• Moving work folders between servers while keeping who can open them.\nOn the same disk, moving is just a change of place and everything is kept.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_DeleteIncomplete_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>“Stop” cancels the transfer and removes it from the list. While copying, each file is written with the .mcpart extension and only gets its name when it finishes correctly.\n• On: pressing Stop deletes the .mcpart files and leaves the destination clean.\n• Off: they are kept, and if you copy the same again it continues right where it left off.\nNothing is ever deleted when closing MegaCopier or after a power cut: those transfers are offered to continue when you open it again.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Eject_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When finished, MegaCopier safely ejects the destination USB stick, card or USB drive, like &quot;Safely remove hardware&quot;.\n• Filling several USB sticks in a row: when you pull one out, it is ready.\nIt only does so if no other copy is using it. &quot;Force&quot; ejects it even if some program has it open.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_OnError_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What to do when a file can’t be copied even after retrying.\n• Ask: the copy stops, tells you why and you choose to retry, skip it, skip the following ones or stop.\n• Skip and continue: ideal for big overnight copies; at the end you see the list of failures.\n• Stop the copy: when nothing may be missing.\n• Retry at the end: leaves it for last, in case it was in use and gets freed.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_ErrorReport_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>If a copy ends with errors, it saves a text file with each file that failed, the reason and the time.\n• To calmly review a copy you left running overnight.\n• To pass it on to whoever looks after the computers.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_MaxJobs_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How many copies can run at the same time. Those using the same disk always wait their turn, because copying two things at once on a hard drive slows everything down.\n• 0 (no limit): every disk works at full speed in parallel.\n• 1: everything in a row, one after another.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Fast_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Drives that handle several copies at once without slowing down, such as NVMe SSDs or RAID. The ones you list here don’t wait their turn.\n• If you copy from a fast SSD to several destinations at the same time.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Slow_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>If you touch the keyboard or mouse, copies drop to the speed you choose so the computer doesn’t feel sluggish. When you stop using it, they go full speed again.\n• Big copies while you work, play or are on video calls.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_ScheduleLimit_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Between the hours you choose, copies don’t go over that speed; outside those hours they go full speed.\n• At the office, so the network isn’t saturated during working hours.\n• At home, so video calls don’t drop in the afternoon.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Priority_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>How much preference Windows gives MegaCopier over other programs.\n• Low: other programs stay smooth; the copy may take a bit longer.\n• Normal: as usual.\n• High: the copy comes first; useful when all you care about is finishing as soon as possible.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Tune_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The first time you copy to a disk, MegaCopier tries several block sizes and numbers of simultaneous operations and keeps the fastest for that disk. It remembers it for later copies.\n• SSDs, hard drives, USB sticks and network drives perform very differently: this way each one runs at its best speed.\nIf you change performance by hand, your choice is respected.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Peer_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Send and receive files between computers running MegaCopier on the same network, without sharing folders or setting anything up. The sender types this computer’s code; everything is encrypted and each file is checked on arrival.\n• Moving photos or videos from the laptop to the desktop PC.\n• Sending a project to a colleague at the office.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Remote_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Opens a page in your phone’s browser (on the same Wi-Fi network) to see how copies are going and pause, resume or cancel them.\n• Leave a long copy running and keep an eye on it from the couch.\nNothing needs installing on the phone; the address and code appear here.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_LiveSync_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>MegaCopier watches a folder and, every time you change something, copies it to another (another disk, a USB stick, a network folder) as soon as you finish saving it.\n• Always having an up-to-date copy of your documents on another disk.\n• Carrying a project’s changes over to a shared folder.\nIn mirror mode, what you delete is also deleted from the copy.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Tasks_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>A saved copy that Windows runs by itself every day, week or month at the time you choose, even if MegaCopier is closed. Only new or changed files are copied.\n• Back up Documents every night.\n• Move photos to the NAS every Sunday.\nCreate it from a copy’s Options tab with &quot;Save as task&quot;.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Guard_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Disks can silently damage files over the years. MegaCopier stores each file’s fingerprint inside it and, when the computer is idle, checks them again. If one changed without anyone touching it, it warns you in time to restore it from a backup.\n• Family photos and videos.\n• Backup disks that are rarely opened.\nWhat you edit updates by itself. Requires NTFS disks.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Webhook_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>When each copy finishes, MegaCopier sends the summary to a web address, so the notification reaches your phone or a chat.\n• Discord, Slack or Teams: notices in a channel.\n• ntfy.sh: a phone notification with almost nothing to install.\n• Home Assistant: automations (turn on a light when done…).</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Connections_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Save the servers you copy to here: FTP, SFTP, WebDAV (Nextcloud, NAS…) or S3 (Backblaze, Wasabi, R2…). They then appear when choosing the source or destination.\n• Upload backups to the cloud.\n• Download files from a company server.\nPasswords are stored in Windows Credential Manager, never as plain text.</value></data>
