  <data name="Info_WhatCopy_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>What is copied from each file</value></data>
  <data name="Info_WhatCopy_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Besides the content, you can copy what Windows keeps around each file.\n• Dates: the creation and last-modified dates stay as in the original, so photos keep their order by date.\n• Attributes: read-only, hidden, archive… Without them, everything arrives as a normal file.\n• Alternate data streams (ADS): hidden data some programs attach to a file, such as the “downloaded from the internet” mark. They&apos;re rarely needed.\n• Security (NTFS): permissions (who can open it), owner and auditing. They only help between NTFS drives on the same computer or domain; owner and auditing require administrator rights.\nExample: for an exact copy of a shared office folder, tick everything; to move photos to a USB stick, Dates is enough.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Transfer_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>How the copy is made</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Transfer_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>• Mode: Copy is the usual one. Sync (mirror) makes the destination identical to the source and removes what&apos;s left over. Update only copies just the files that already exist in the destination and have changed. Folder structure only creates the empty folders. Organize photos by date sorts them into year and month folders.\n• Simulate: tells you what would be done without changing anything. Try it before a mirror.\n• If the file already exists: ask, replace, skip, keep the newer one, keep both…\n• Hash: how the copy is checked to be identical. xxHash is the fastest; SHA-256 is the standard for keeping proof.\n• Buffer size and In parallel: bigger and more reads at once speed up SSDs and networks; on hard disks or slow USB sticks, 1 or 2 is better.\n• Verify after copying: rereads what was copied and compares it with the original. It takes longer, but you know it&apos;s right.\n• Use the system cache: faster with thousands of small files; without it, huge files don&apos;t fill up memory.\nExample: to move 500 GB of videos to a new drive with complete safety, choose Copy, tick Verify and leave 4 in parallel.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_OnFinish_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>When the copy finishes</value></data>
  <data name="Info_OnFinish_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What MegaCopier leaves done when it finishes.\n• Save checksum file: a file in the destination with the fingerprint (hash) of every copied file. Later, “Check checksums” uses it to tell you whether anything has gone bad on the drive.\n• Save HTML report: a summary that opens in the browser, with what was copied, the time, the speed and the errors. Handy to keep or send.\n• Commands when finished: commands run at the end (see their own help).\nExample: when moving the family photos to an external drive, save the checksum file: a year from now you&apos;ll know whether any of them got damaged.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Scripts_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Commands when finished</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Scripts_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Windows commands (the cmd.exe ones) that run when the copy finishes, one per line.\n• Start the line with “ok:” so it only runs if everything went well, or with “error:” if something failed. With nothing in front, it always runs.\n• You can use these variables: %MEGACOPIER_DESTINATION% (destination folder), %MEGACOPIER_SOURCES% (sources), %MEGACOPIER_STATE% (how it ended), %MEGACOPIER_FILES% (files copied), %MEGACOPIER_BYTES% (bytes), %MEGACOPIER_FAILED% (failures) and %MEGACOPIER_CHECKSUM% (checksum file).\nExamples:\n• ok: explorer &quot;%MEGACOPIER_DESTINATION%&quot; opens the folder when done.\n• ok: shutdown /s /t 60 turns the computer off a minute later.\n• error: msg * The copy had %MEGACOPIER_FAILED% failures warns you with a message.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Filters_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Filters: copy only part of it</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Filters_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Copy only what you care about. What&apos;s left out isn&apos;t copied or touched in the destination.\n• Say it in your own words: type, for example, “photos over 2 MB from last month without the .tmp files” and MegaCopier fills in the filters for you.\n• Include only: masks separated by semicolons. “*.jpg; *.png” copies only images; “Photos\*” only what&apos;s in folders called Photos.\n• Exclude: the same, the other way round. For example “node_modules; *.tmp; Thumbs.db”.\n• Minimum and maximum size (MB): with a minimum of 100 you copy only the big videos.\n• Modified from / until: only what changed in those dates, for example since Monday.\n• Skip hidden, system and junk files (Thumbs.db, desktop.ini…).\n• Only images with some text: Windows OCR reads the photos and copies only those that contain it, such as “invoice”.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Rules_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Rules for special cases</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Rules_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>What to do when something isn&apos;t a normal file or fails.\n• Symbolic links and junctions: copy them as links (as in the source), copy what&apos;s behind them (their real content) or skip them.\n• Name when keeping both: what the new copy is called when you choose to keep both files. {name} is the name, {n} a number, {ext} the extension and {date} the date: “{name} ({n}){ext}” gives “photo (2).jpg”.\n• Retries and delay: if a file fails (the network drops, another program has it open…), how many times it&apos;s retried and how many seconds to wait between attempts.\n• If a file keeps failing: skip it and go on, stop the copy, leave it for the end or ask you.\nExample: when copying from a NAS over Wi-Fi, set 5 retries with a 10-second delay: short drops won&apos;t spoil the copy.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Safety_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>Sync and safety</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Safety_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Options to sync folders and protect your data. They&apos;re all optional; hover over each one to see more.\n• If a folder already exists: merge its contents, skip it or ask.\n• Keep what&apos;s replaced or removed: before overwriting or deleting a file, it&apos;s moved to that folder with the date (for example .versions), so you can get the previous version back. “How many versions to keep” cleans out the old ones.\n• Other places with the same data: if the data is also somewhere else (a second NAS), big files are read from both at once.\n• Date tolerance: avoids copying identical files again on FAT32 or exFAT USB sticks.\n• Checks: reread the original, store the hash inside the file and recovery data to repair the copy if the drive damages it.\n• Protection: ransomware shield, forensic mode, secure delete when moving and “all or nothing” folders.\n• Difficult drives: wait if the drive disconnects or space runs out, rescue mode for damaged drives and delta copy of big files.\nExample: for a weekly backup with Sync, set a versions folder and tick “Reread the original”.</value></data>
