  <data name="Settings_BackdropTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Window background</value></data>
  <data name="Settings_BackdropDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>The Windows 11 material shown behind the window, with any theme.</value></data>
  <data name="Backdrop_OptTheme.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>The theme&apos;s own</value></data>
  <data name="Backdrop_OptTranslucent.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Translucent (desktop shows through)</value></data>
  <data name="BackdropAcrylicThin.Content" xml:space="preserve"><value>Thin acrylic</value></data>
  <data name="Info_Backdrop_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>It&apos;s what shows behind the window&apos;s content.\n• The theme&apos;s own: each theme brings its own (solid, Mica or acrylic). Fluent themes use Mica, which switches to Mica Alt while you use another window instead of turning a solid color.\n• Mica: gently takes on the tint of your desktop wallpaper. It&apos;s the one Windows 11 apps use.\n• Mica Alt: like Mica, but with more color from the wallpaper.\n• Acrylic: like frosted glass; whatever is behind the window looks blurred.\n• Translucent: lighter, more transparent acrylic; the desktop shows through the window.\nIf you pick one of these four, the window turns a solid color while you use another one, like Windows&apos; own windows. MegaCopier&apos;s menus and option lists are always acrylic.\nMica and acrylic need Windows 11 with transparency effects turned on; otherwise you&apos;ll see a solid color.</value></data>
  <data name="SameDrive_NewWindow" xml:space="preserve"><value>In a new window</value></data>
  <data name="SameDrive_NewWindowText" xml:space="preserve"><value>Another MegaCopier window opens with this copy and it starts right away, alongside the other one (both will be somewhat slower).</value></data>
  <data name="Settings_IndependentWindowsTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Each copy in its own window</value></data>
  <data name="Settings_IndependentWindowsDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>One window per copy, starting right away without waiting for the others. No copy list, unless you add several to the same window.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_IndependentWindows_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Normally MegaCopier has a single window and, if you start several copies, it shows them in its copy list. With this option, each copy opens its own window and starts right away, without waiting for the others.\n• They&apos;re all the same app: theme, icons and settings are shared (change them in one and they change in all).\n• Each window has its own progress icon in the tray and its own button on the taskbar.\n• If the new copy goes to the same drive as one that is running, &quot;New copy to the same drive&quot; decides: ask (add it to that window&apos;s copy or open it in a new window), always add it, or always open a new window without asking.\n• If you add copies to a window, that window shows its own copy list.\n• When its copies finish, each window closes as usual.\nExample: you copy photos to a USB drive and, meanwhile, videos to another disk: you&apos;ll see two windows, each with its own progress.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_CloseButton_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>When you click the window&apos;s X</value></data>
  <data name="Info_CloseButton_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Choose what happens to your copies when you close the window with the X.\n• Cancel the copies: like pressing “Stop” on each one. They disappear from the list and aren&apos;t offered to continue.\n• Stop them to continue later: what was copied is kept; when you copy the same thing again, or open MegaCopier, it picks up where it was.\n• Hide the window: the copies keep going in the background; open it from the tray icon.\n• Exit MegaCopier: it closes completely and Windows copies as usual until you open it again.</value></data>
  <data name="Info_MinimizeButton_Title" xml:space="preserve"><value>When you minimize the window</value></data>
  <data name="Info_MinimizeButton_Text" xml:space="preserve"><value>Where the window goes when you minimize it. Your copies keep going either way.\n• To the taskbar: like any other window.\nMinimizing to the tray keeps the taskbar free: the window comes back with a click on the MegaCopier icon next to the clock.</value></data>
